@title The Baldwin Effect Needs a Selective Bridge
@sigils [🌱/化]
@keywords baldwin, learning, evolution, plasticity, genetic-assimilation, canalization, metaca, mechanism
@audience evolutionary-computation researchers, MetaCA experiment designers, futon theorists
@tone analytic-mechanistic
@style argument
@hasa [baldwin/two-claims-not-one baldwin/plasticity-builds-a-selective-neighbourhood baldwin/assimilable-traits-need-heritable-shadows baldwin/assimilation-requires-a-resolved-path baldwin/stationarity-decides-what-can-be-fixed baldwin/metaca-negative-evidence-localizes-the-bottleneck baldwin/test-evolution-of-learnability-before-static-assimilation]
@references [futon-theory/baldwin-cycle eight-gates/press-mechanism exotic/hybrid-execution-semantics]
@references-extra ["Hinton and Nowlan (1987), How Learning Can Guide Evolution, Complex Systems 1:495-502" "Crispo (2007), The Baldwin Effect and Genetic Assimilation, Evolution 61:2469-2479" "Aguilar, Bennati and Helbing (2019), How learning can change the course of evolution, PLOS ONE 14:e0219502" "Gupta et al. (2021), Embodied Intelligence via Learning and Evolution, Nature 592:545-552" "Wong et al. (2023), Evolutionary Optimization of Physics-Informed Neural Networks, arXiv:2312.03243"]

! conclusion:
  Lifetime plasticity can guide evolution when it makes inherited differences
  predictably visible to selection, but genetic assimilation is a further result,
  not the definition of the Baldwin effect. Assimilation additionally requires a
  stable target, inherited variables that are expressed while plasticity remains,
  reachable substitutions that preserve function, and fitness resolution strong
  enough to retain those substitutions. A negative at any one gate localizes the
  missing bridge; it does not refute Baldwin effects in general.

  + context:
    `futon-theory/baldwin-cycle` names the desired engineering progression from
    exploration to assimilation to canalization. Hinton and Nowlan establish a
    particular causal construction: fixed correct alleles reduce the number of
    lifetime guesses needed to reach one invariant target. The MetaCA experiments
    instead ask whether an initially random rule can be held while a contextual
    rewriting process remains functional. These are not automatically the same
    evolutionary geometry.

  + IF:
    A system has inherited variables, within-lifetime adaptation, differential
    reproductive success, and a proposed measure of reduced plastic dependence.

  + HOWEVER:
    Learning may guide inherited biases while remaining permanently useful; a good
    static endpoint may exist but be unreachable; a mutation may remove plasticity
    before installing a useful inherited substitute; a changing task may select for
    plasticity; and a cost proxy may be too coarse or too noisy to create selection.

    + FAILURE-MODES:
      - Calling every reduction in plasticity a Baldwin effect.
      - Requiring complete loss of learning before recognizing evolution of learnability.
      - Treating existence of rule 90 as evidence of a path from the evolved plastic lineage.
      - Treating failure of random hold mutations as failure of genetic assimilation.
      - Changing representation, cost, target, and mutation operator together and losing diagnosis.

  + THEN:
    Separate two claims and test them in order. First test guidance: does lifetime
    rewriting create selection for inherited fields or update biases that improve
    learning speed, early performance, or held-out post-learning performance relative
    to evolution without rewriting? Only then test strong assimilation: can function
    be retained along an accessible path whose realized learning work declines and
    whose endpoint works without rewriting?

    + CHECK:
      Require a no-learning evolutionary comparison for guidance; a mutation-only
      lifecycle null for hold frequencies; an explicit accessible path to a functional
      inherited endpoint; treatment-separated rankings; aligned random tapes; and
      evaluation on preregistered training and held-out task distributions.

  + BECAUSE:
    Plasticity changes evolution only through selection on inherited variation. It
    helps when inherited changes alter the probability, speed, reliability, or cost of
    reaching a useful phenotype. Assimilation occurs only when selection can replace
    some of that adaptive work with an inherited cause without crossing a fitness
    valley larger than the saving.

    + MECHANISM:
      phenotype search -> differential fitness among inherited starting points or
      learning biases -> accumulation of variants that reach useful phenotypes more
      cheaply -> optional reduction of plastic work when inherited substitutes become
      sufficient.

    + COUNTERFACTUAL:
      If inherited variation does not affect post-learning success, if fixation exposes
      dormant random alleles, or if every local reduction of rewriting destroys
      function, plasticity may remain load-bearing indefinitely even though Baldwinian
      guidance is possible in other representations.

    + EVIDENCE:
      Hinton and Nowlan's `?` alleles create a high-fitness neighbourhood around one
      target. Recent morphological and neural work likewise evolves bodies,
      initializations, or learning biases that make lifetime learning faster; it does
      not generally require learning to vanish. The MetaCA pilot retained reach
      12.44--13.00 while dependence stayed 0.961--0.984 and produced no registered
      static witness, identifying the strong-assimilation bridge as absent in the
      tested representation and search budget.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Use the child patterns as the causal argument attached to future Lean registrations.
    - Preregister a guidance experiment before another complete-assimilation search.
    - Keep the existing strict `BaldwinWitness` as the certificate for the stronger claim.
